  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "tip",
      "title": "After this PDF you can",
      "bullets": [
        "form the present subjunctive of regular verbs in every person",
        "form it for verbs with irregular yo forms, stem changes and spelling changes",
        "recognise the six verbs that are fully irregular (ser, estar, ir, haber, saber, dar)",
        "see when Spanish needs que + subjunctive (the uses are in PDF 3)"
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "1. What is the subjunctive?"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "So far you have used the <b>indicative</b> (present, preterite, imperfect, future ...), which states facts. The <b>subjunctive</b> is a different <b>mood</b>: it is used in a subordinate clause (after <i>que</i>) for wishes, requests, emotions, doubt and things that are not yet real."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "style": "plain",
      "widths": [
        5.0,
        6.0,
        6.0
      ],
      "head": [
        "Indicative (fact)",
        "Subjunctive (wish, doubt, emotion)",
        "English"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Sé que vienes.",
          "Quiero que vengas.",
          "I know you are coming. / I want you to come."
        ],
        [
          "Creo que tiene tiempo.",
          "Espero que tenga tiempo.",
          "I think he has time. / I hope he has time."
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "dutch",
      "title": "Dutch uses the plain present here",
      "text": "<i>Ik wil dat je komt</i> uses the normal present in Dutch. Spanish needs <b>Quiero que vengas</b>. Whenever you say <i>dat</i> after a verb of wanting, hoping or feeling, think subjunctive."
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "tip",
      "title": "Same subject: infinitive. Different subject: que + subjunctive",
      "text": "<i>Quiero comer.</i> (I want to eat: same person.) <i>Quiero que comas.</i> (I want you to eat: different person.) This pattern is the key to the next PDF."
    },
    {
      "type": "h1",
      "text": "2. Forming the present subjunctive"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Take the <b>yo</b> form of the present tense, remove <b>-o</b>, and add the <b>opposite vowel</b>: <b>-ar</b> verbs take <b>e</b>, <b>-er / -ir</b> verbs take <b>a</b>. These are the same forms you already know from the usted and negative commands (A2 PDF 7)."
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "style": "plain",
      "widths": [
        5.0,
        4.0,
        4.0,
        4.0
      ],
      "head": [
        "Person",
        "-ar (hablar)",
        "-er (comer)",
        "-ir (vivir)"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "yo",
          "-e",
          "-a",
          "-a"
        ],
        [
          "tú",
          "-es",
          "-as",
          "-as"
        ],
        [
          "él / ella / usted",
          "-e",
          "-a",
          "-a"
        ],
        [
          "nosotros / nosotras",
          "-emos",
          "-amos",
          "-amos"
        ],
        [
          "vosotros / vosotras",
          "-éis",
          "-áis",
          "-áis"
        ],
        [
          "ellos / ellas / ustedes",
          "-en",
          "-an",
          "-an"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "kind": "tip",
      "title": "Why the irregular yo forms matter",
      "text": "Because you start from the yo form, <i>tengo</i> gives <b>tenga</b>, <i>hago</i> gives <b>haga</b>, <i>conozco</i> gives <b>conozca</b>. Yo and él / ella / usted have the <b>same form</b>: <i>que yo hable</i> and <i>que él hable</i>."
    },
